## 7. Exit criteria for the epic

This epic is complete when the following are all true:

- Every priority runtime path is reconstructible through a stable trace contract rather than scattered logs alone.[2]
- Runtime components follow explicit ownership boundaries that match Kernel and Runtime Context Protocol semantics.[1][3]
- Evaluation is trace-linked and version-aware.[6][2]
- Failure taxonomy is stable enough for recurrence analysis and postmortems.[2]
- Cost and billable outcomes are attributable by workspace, workflow, and department.[2]
- Agent Company workflows do not bypass core runtime, policy, or observability infrastructure.[1][3]

***

## 8. Immediate next actions

1. Harden `TraceStore v1` and complete `trace_id` propagation first; governance without traceability remains theoretical at runtime.[1][2]
2. Make failure taxonomy mandatory in planner/orchestrator/tool/memory failure paths.[2]
3. Link Evaluator outputs to traces and runtime versions before expanding autonomy or commercial workflow complexity.[6][2]
4. Add minimal billable-event support only after trace and cost attribution are reliable enough to explain workflow economics.[3][2]
5. Build the first operator-facing trace/failure/cost views on top of the stabilized contracts, not before.[5][2]